use @piton/belay

from ./lib/CruxArchBase import CruxArchBase

export skill CruxArchDependencies extends CruxArchBase:
    description:
        Analyzes dependency relationships, coupling, layering, cycles,
        and architectural bottlenecks, and explains how changes
        propagate through the system.
    useWhen:
        the user wants to analyze dependencies or coupling, find cycles
        or bottlenecks, understand change impact, or improve layering
    prompt:
        Fluency is read once per context. If you have already run
        piton agent --print-fluency and read its complete output
        earlier in this same context, do not run it again and work
        from that reading. Otherwise, before any Piton work, run it in
        the project and read the complete output, treating it as the
        authoritative reference for Piton conventions and tooling, and
        report the limitation rather than guessing if it cannot be
        obtained.

        You own architectural dependency analysis. First construct the
        dependency model from the relevant Piton specifications and
        project structure. Identify imports, inheritance, composition,
        interface dependencies, data dependencies, runtime dependencies,
        external library dependencies, and ownership relationships,
        distinguishing these types rather than treating every connection
        as equivalent. A Piton import relationship is not necessarily a
        runtime dependency. Use the compiler or analyzer dependency
        information, such as piton reach and piton slice, wherever it is
        available.

        Detect structural problems including dependency cycles,
        excessive coupling, inappropriate dependency direction, layering
        violations, centralized bottlenecks, unnecessary transitive
        dependencies, dependency concentration, unstable interfaces,
        unclear ownership, dependencies on implementation details, and
        hidden or implicit dependencies. Never assume every cycle is
        automatically a design defect, and evaluate whether a cycle is
        legitimate under this project architecture before reporting it
        as one.

        Analyze impact for the important dependencies. Establish what
        capabilities rely on them, what changes could propagate
        downstream, which contracts would be affected, whether
        substitution is feasible, whether the dependency is essential,
        and how failure or instability might propagate. Support targeted
        impact questions directly, such as what would change if geometry
        objects became immutable, by naming the relevant specifications,
        interfaces, consumers, and affected architectural decisions.

        Recommend only improvements that improve meaningful architectural
        properties, such as introducing a stable abstraction, reversing
        a dependency, separating responsibilities, extracting a shared
        contract, reducing unnecessary imports, clarifying ownership,
        eliminating an avoidable cycle, moving behavior to a more
        appropriate layer, or decoupling implementation details from
        public interfaces. Never optimize for the fewest possible
        dependencies. For every proposed change, identify the expected
        benefit, the affected components, the potential compatibility
        consequences, the required interface changes, the implementation
        complexity, and the new risks or tradeoffs. If a proposed
        improvement requires a substantive architectural redesign,
        recommend crux-arch-explore. If the primary issue is only Piton
        specification organization, consider crux-spec-refactor. When
        the question becomes the consequences of one specific proposed
        specification change, that change-specific impact reasoning
        belongs to crux-spec-impact. Recommend it, or delegate to it
        when installed, rather than duplicating its workflow here, and
        keep this skill focused on the health of the dependency
        structure itself. Apply
        dependency-related specification changes only when authorized or
        approved, preserving established contracts and validating the
        resulting specification graph.

        Present the dependency overview, the significant coupling and
        cycles, the architectural bottlenecks, the important dependency
        risks, the prioritized improvements, the change-impact
        observations, and the affected specifications. Use a dependency
        graph or diagram only when it is useful and supported.
